  "query_preview": "..\nA weak line of convergence associated with a broad trough centered\nnear Palau and easterly trade winds is sitting over the Marianas and\nbringing scattered showers to Guam tonight with isolated \nthunderstorms for all islands. The risk for isolated thunderstorms \nwill continue through Monday morning for Guam as an upper-level \ntrough moves into the region and wind shear increases significantly. \nHowever, a diffusive low-level flow and dry upper-level air will \nlikely keep storm coverage isolated. By Monday, the upper-level \ntrough will start to move out and only isolated showers are \nanticipated until Friday night. As winds decrease over the next \ncouple of days, the one exception may be for scattered island-effect\nshowers in the afternoon over Guam Tuesday and Wednesday. Current \nTropical Storm In-Fa is expected to make it's closest point of \napproach sometime Friday or Saturday which will bring scattered \nshowers for the CNMI and potentially breezy conditions. This \nsituation remains highly uncertain, though most models show a passage\nthrough the remote, far northern CNMI with minimal impacts to the \nMarianas. This will continue to be monitored closely for any \npotential changes. See the \"Tropical Systems\" section for more \ndetails.",
